The concurrent builder is still active. Its replacement is not a superficial edit: it uses the same \(k=4\) Frobenius block but composes it with invertible blocks to obtain an \(88\times88\) kernel-certificate problem, raising the mechanical/reference cost above one million operations while keeping the compact route at 203. That better matches Track B’s intended compression gap than my 16-variable version, so I’m preserving it and independently validating its mathematics and gates rather than reverting it. The README/report will be regenerated only after that process settles.
