+NOTES = r"""
+Paper definition.  Section 2 defines a q-polynomial (linearized polynomial)
+f(X)=sum a_i X^(q^i), identifies these maps with matrices over GF(q), and
+states that their rank equals the rank of the associated Dickson matrix
+(Lemma 2.3).  Theorem 1.2 constructs T_{n,s,eta}; its proof rewrites every
+bilinear form as Tr(y g(x)), so the form and g have the same rank.  This module
+keeps those native finite-field linear maps rather than replacing them by a
+graph or finite combinatorial surrogate.
+
+Step-0 discrimination.  The prior proposal to enumerate all codewords and all
+pairwise rank distances was discarded.  T_{n,s,eta} has q^(2n) words, and the
+paper proves the rank bound by Dickson minors, explicitly saying that the n=8
+and n=10 calculations required Maple.  Conversely, a kernel certificate for
+one displayed operator is produced by ordinary modular Gaussian elimination.
+That polynomial-time algorithm disqualifies Track A but creates an honest
+Track B gap: a dense N by N presentation costs O(N^3), whereas the planted
+normal-basis/change-of-variables route costs O(N).
+
+Construction from the paper.  Fix the allowed case k=4, n=8, s=1 from
+Theorem 1.2 and set b0=b2=0 and b1=c in GF(p).  Equation (5) in Section 3 then
+gives g=c(F^3+F^5), where F(x)=x^p.  In a normal basis F cyclically shifts eight
+coordinates.  Thus g(x)=0 is x_i+x_{i+2}=0 around the orbit, with the two
+independent alternating solutions
+  (1,0,-1,0,1,0,-1,0) and (0,1,0,-1,0,1,0,-1).
+The paper's Lemma 3.3 also bounds this kernel by dimension two.  Other blocks
+use b0=c and b1=b2=0, hence g=cF^4 and are invertible.  Their direct sum has
+exactly the two known null vectors.
+
+Before the instance is emitted, generation samples u,v with v^T u=0 and with
+v pairing to 1 against both alternating vectors.  Therefore P=I+uv^T is
+invertible with P^(-1)=I-uv^T.  It emits M=P L P^(-1) and carries the known
+kernel basis to PZ=Z+u[1,1].  This is inverse generation plus composition of
+identities; no elimination, root search, or rank search produces the answer.
+
+Reference route and compact route.  Exact modular Gaussian elimination is the
+domain-standard algorithm, always succeeds, and is reported outside attacks as
+Track B requires.  The compact route recognizes the unique paired Frobenius
+orbit, writes its two alternating vectors, observes their sparse dot products
+with v are one, and adds u to each.  At shipping size this is conservatively
+counted as 2N+16+(N/8)=203 exact operations.
+
+Adversaries.  Dense rank-one conjugation destroys raw alternating vectors,
+standard-coordinate outliers, and low-weight-column guesses.  The auxiliary
+vectors themselves are not null vectors.  Uniform restarts sample the exact
+language of ordered independent column pairs.  All attacks are rerun on eight
+shipping seeds; successful Gaussian elimination is separately disclosed.
+
+Canonicalization.  Coordinate relabeling, Frobenius-orbit rotation, and ledger
+reordering are normalized away.  The key uses the prime and the sorted multiset
+of block types and nonzero scalars, which are invariant under all generated
+changes of basis.  This is a strong invariant for this direct-sum family, not a
+general canonical form for arbitrary linear operators.
+""".strip()
+
+
+_ANSWER_RE = re.compile(r"<answer\b[^>]*>(.*?)</answer\s*>", re.I | re.S)
+_G4_SAMPLES = 200_000
+_ATTACK_SEEDS = 8
